WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.430
The librarian asked: which class

2
00:00:03.430 --> 00:00:05.910
borrowed the most books this term?

3
00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:10.420
She has eleven thousand loan records.

4
00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:13.310
You'll answer it in five lines.

5
00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:16.880
Today: asking real questions in

6
00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.040
SQL, from WHERE to GROUP BY.

7
00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:22.430
WHERE keeps the rows you want,

8
00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:24.850
like class equals five B.

9
00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:29.460
ORDER BY sorts them, DESC for the biggest

10
00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:32.100
first, and LIMIT keeps the top few.

11
00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:36.070
To count per class, GROUP BY makes one

12
00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:38.910
row for each class, and COUNT star

13
00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:40.630
counts the rows in each group.

14
00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.970
Sort by that count, keep one row,

15
00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:45.530
and there's your answer.

16
00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:51.100
COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN and MAX are

17
00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:54.360
aggregates: they turn many rows into one

18
00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:54.740
value.

19
00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:58.530
They skip NULL, the empty value, except

20
00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:00.930
COUNT star, which counts every row.

21
00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:02.590
Quick check!

22
00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:05.710
COUNT star says twelve, but

23
00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:07.810
COUNT of mark says eleven.

24
00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:08.740
Why?

25
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.840
One mark is NULL: someone missed the test.

26
00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:16.420
COUNT star counts the row; COUNT of

27
00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:18.240
mark skips the empty value.

28
00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:22.100
What does GROUP BY do inside?

29
00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:25.240
Think of a dictionary: one key per

30
00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:27.420
class, and a running count for each.

31
00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:31.190
Here it is in Python, reading a CSV file.

32
00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:33.840
Always judge the answer.

33
00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:36.070
Do the class counts add up

34
00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:37.030
to the school's total?

35
00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:39.990
Is any group impossibly big?

36
00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:42.620
Test your query on a few rows

37
00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:43.800
you can count by hand.

38
00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:47.020
In the app, real SQLite

39
00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:48.040
runs on your phone.

40
00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:50.570
Run the examples, then change

41
00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:54.630
them: switch AVG to MAX, or ask

42
00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:56.210
which pupils live in Hilltop.

43
00:01:57.470 --> 00:01:58.210
Your turn.

44
00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:01.470
Which line keeps only the

45
00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:03.970
rows for class five B?

46
00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:07.870
WHERE class equals five B,

47
00:02:08.390 --> 00:02:09.030
in single quotes.

48
00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:13.870
Remember: WHERE picks rows, ORDER BY

49
00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:16.650
sorts, and LIMIT keeps a few.

50
00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:19.790
GROUP BY makes one row per group,

51
00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:22.070
and aggregates skip NULL.

52
00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:24.880
And test every query on rows

53
00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:26.120
you can check by hand.

54
00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:30.250
Try it tomorrow: write three questions

55
00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:32.630
you'd ask your school library's database.

56
00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:37.700
Then write each one in SQL: SELECT,

57
00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:42.000
FROM, WHERE, and maybe GROUP BY.
